\aB{...}
\aC{...}
```

Preserve their present semantics.

In particular:

* `\aA{}` uses the default text color, which should switch appropriately between the black-ish light-mode color and white-ish dark-mode color;
* `\aB{}` is red;
* `\aC{}` is blue.

If there are additional commentary authors/styles, model them systematically.

Again, this is a natural candidate for a Lua-backed profile table with thin LaTeX wrappers.

---

## 14. Word-level configuration and macros

Finally we reach the level of individual words.

Put configurable lexical macros here.

This includes things such as:

```latex
\God
```

and similar macros where the spelling, typography, wording, or presentation of a particular recurring word is intentionally configurable.

Also put the redaction jokes and related word-level machinery here, including things like:

```latex
\cum
\pussy
```

and whatever other such macros exist.

For example, if one currently expands through machinery resembling:

```latex
\c\redacted{um}\eR{hildren}
```

preserve the resulting behavior while simplifying the implementation where possible.

Do not sanitize, editorialize, rename, or remove these merely because they are unusual. They are part of the existing book design.

At this point we are at the smallest meaningful reusable semantic unit.

I do not believe there is significant reusable infrastructure below the word level except font/glyph machinery, which belongs much earlier under fonts.

