# 1. Implementation advice: exploit LuaLaTeX rather than fighting TeX

The architectural motivation for this rewrite is that LuaLaTeX gives us several ways to escape the increasingly tangled TeX-programming style in `master.tex`.

There are essentially three levels available.

---

## Level 1: use `expl3` when the state genuinely belongs in TeX

Modern LaTeX's `expl3` layer provides considerably saner TeX-side programming facilities than primitive TeX conditionals.

It has:

* sequences;
* property maps;
* token lists;
* booleans;
* integers;
* floating-point support;
* structured conditionals;
* useful string/token operations.

For example, instead of constructing lookup behavior out of `\ifx`, one can use a property map:

```latex
\ExplSyntaxOn

\prop_new:N \g_book_prop

\prop_gput:Nnn \g_book_prop { Genesis } { J }
\prop_gput:Nnn \g_book_prop { Exodus  } { P }
\prop_gput:Nnn \g_book_prop { Samuel }  { J }

\prop_get:NnN \g_book_prop { Genesis } \l_tmpa_tl

\tl_if_eq:NnTF \l_tmpa_tl { J }
  { This~is~J. }
  { This~is~not~J. }

\ExplSyntaxOff
```

Booleans can similarly be expressed sanely:

```latex
\bool_new:N \l_show_notes_bool
\bool_set_true:N \l_show_notes_bool

\bool_if:NTF \l_show_notes_bool
  { Show the notes }
  { Hide the notes }
```

So where a piece of state genuinely belongs to TeX, prefer `expl3` abstractions to primitive TeX programming.

However, **do not respond to this task merely by rewriting all primitive TeX in `expl3`.**

That would improve syntax while retaining the deeper architectural problem.

For substantial structured metadata, Lua is preferable.

---

# Level 2: use Lua tables as actual data structures

LuaLaTeX embeds a real general-purpose programming language.

Use actual Lua tables for structured data.

For example:

```latex
\directlua{
  books = {
    Genesis = {
      source = "J",
      color = "red",
      chapters = 50
    },

    Exodus = {
      source = "mixed",
      color = "blue",
      chapters = 40
    }
  }
}
```

Then expose small accessors to TeX where needed:

```latex
\newcommand{\BookSource}[1]{%
  \directlua{tex.sprint(books["#1"].source)}%
}
```

so that:

```latex
\BookSource{Genesis}
```

can simply yield:

```text
J
```

The actual project can and should use much richer structures where appropriate.

For example, conceptually:

```lua
verses = {
    ["Gen 1:1"] = {
        source = "P",
        hebrew = "...",
        english = "...",
        notes = {"creation", "syntax"},
        special = true
    },

    ["Gen 14:13"] = {
        source = "Other",
        special = true,
        style = "stele"
    }
}
```

Likewise, author profiles, font mappings, source categories, colors, book metadata, ToC data, and similar structures are natural Lua-table candidates.

The main principle is:

> If the TeX currently contains a pile of macros whose real purpose is to simulate a dictionary, record, list, set, enum, or nested configuration object, replace that pile with an actual Lua data structure.

---

# Level 3: don't make TeX perform complicated conditionals at all

This is often the strongest option.

Instead of writing increasingly elaborate TeX such as:

```latex
\ifx...
   ...
\else
   ...
\fi
```

write the decision logic in Lua and have Lua emit the appropriate TeX or expose a simple result.

For example:

```latex
\newcommand{\RenderSource}[1]{%
  \directlua{
    local source = "#1"

    if source == "J" then
        tex.sprint("\\eJ")
    elseif source == "P" then
        tex.sprint("\\eP")
    elseif source == "D" then
        tex.sprint("\\eD")
    else
        tex.sprint("\\eOther")
    end
  }%
}
```

Then something conceptually like:

```
```


latex id="x88yj5"
\RenderSource{J}{Some text}

````

can dispatch to the existing source rendering.

Lua gives ordinary programming constructs:

```lua id="q0d83c"
if x == "foo" then
    ...
elseif x == "bar" then
    ...
else
    ...
end
````

as well as:

```lua id="ajgfmu"
if x and y then
```

```lua id="r7f03n"
if x or y then
```

```lua id="ykqsr8"
if not x then
```

```lua id="h0b8s6"
if table[key] ~= nil then
```

```lua id="51sdz9"
if #items > 0 then
```

Use those instead of forcing TeX to be a general-purpose application language.

---

# Preferred division of responsibility

Aim for a hybrid architecture roughly like this:

```text id="5d6td3"
TeX / LaTeX
    ↓
page layout
fonts
paragraphs
boxes
footnotes
cross-references
actual typesetting

expl3
    ↓
small pieces of TeX-local state
simple booleans
macro arguments
temporary lists/properties
TeX-facing glue where useful

Lua
    ↓
real data structures
configuration records
book/chapter/verse metadata
author/source profiles
lookup tables
complicated conditions
loops
string processing
generating repetitive TeX
```

This is not an absolute rule. Use judgment.

But if you encounter something like:

```latex id="jvr4i2"
\ifx\CurrentSource\J
  \ifnum\CurrentChapter>12
    \ifx\SpecialMode\True
       ...
    \else
       ...
    \fi
  \fi
\fi
```

that is a strong signal that the decision belongs in Lua:

```lua id="dxls6i"
if verse.source == "J"
   and verse.chapter > 12
   and verse.special then

    ...
end
```

Lua provides:

* tables;
* functions;
* modules;
* local variables;
* loops;
* ordinary boolean logic;
* string processing;
* pattern matching;
* file I/O;
* sensible numeric operations;
* actual structured programming.

Use it.

---

# Important architectural preference: Lua as model/controller, LaTeX as rendering engine

The project is programmatic enough that the cleanest mental model is probably:

> **Lua is the model/controller. LaTeX is the rendering engine.**

Do not take this so far that every trivial TeX operation requires a Lua round trip.

But use it to guide the architecture.

Lua should know things such as:

* what sources exist;
* how sources are categorized;
* what properties an author has;
* which font profile belongs to whom;
* what books exist;
* what chapter-summary records exist;
* which modes are active;
* mappings and lookup tables;
* configuration data;
* relationships among entities.

LaTeX should know how to turn those decisions into beautiful pages.

The ideal public TeX API should therefore be small.

Chapter files should still be pleasant to write.

For example, existing expressive calls such as:

```latex id="y0wwyp"
\Verse{13}
```

and source-specific content macros should remain concise. Do not "simplify" `mistress.tex` at the cost of making every chapter file verbose or ugly.

Instead, make those commands thin interfaces over a much cleaner implementation.

---

# Preserve compatibility where it buys substantial simplicity elsewhere

Do not assume every existing macro name is sacred, but recognize that the chapter files constitute a large existing corpus.

If preserving a public macro such as:

```latex id="hy02pg"
\Verse
```

```latex id="lnc5ao"
\hJ
```

```latex id="73u6la"
\eJ
```

```latex id="3bzyo1"
\aA
```

allows all existing content files to continue working, preserve it.

The objective is not to produce a theoretically pure API.

The objective is to produce the **simplest maintainable implementation of the existing book**.

A five-line compatibility wrapper is preferable to mechanically editing hundreds of content files for no semantic gain.

---

# Avoid repeated `\directlua` spaghetti

Using Lua does not automatically make the design good.

Do not replace TeX spaghetti with hundreds of tiny inline `\directlua{...}` fragments.

Prefer a coherent Lua layer.

If appropriate, define one or a few substantial Lua tables/modules/functions and expose a small number of generic bridges to TeX.

For example, prefer conceptually:

```lua id="9cfnoc"
Book.author("J")
Book.get("J", "english_font")
Book.render_source("J", ...)
```

over independently embedding arbitrary Lua snippets everywhere.

If putting substantial Lua code in a separate `.lua` file makes `mistress.tex` dramatically cleaner, that is acceptable and probably desirable. `mistress.tex` can load it. The primary deliverable remains `mistress.tex`, but supporting Lua files are welcome if they produce a genuinely cleaner architecture.

Choose names appropriate to the actual project rather than blindly copying these examples.

---

# Be careful at the TeX/Lua boundary

Remember that TeX tokenization, expansion, catcodes, escaping, braces, Unicode, and Lua strings can interact in unpleasant ways.

Do not naïvely interpolate arbitrary Hebrew, commentary, or other content into Lua source strings.

Prefer robust interfaces.

Structured metadata is an excellent thing to keep in Lua.

Large arbitrary TeX content may often be better left as TeX arguments/tokens unless there is a compelling reason to move it.

The goal is not "put everything in Lua."

The goal is:

> Put **data and program logic** where Lua handles them naturally, and leave **typesetting content and rendering** where TeX handles them naturally.

---

# Preserve the painstaking typography

Treat existing values as intentional unless evidence strongly suggests otherwise.

This applies especially to:

* spacing;
* margins;
* vertical skips;
* horizontal alignment;
* title placement;
* alphabet-history layouts;
* source colors;
* dark-mode colors;
* font selection;
* font scaling;
* Hebrew positioning;
* source headers;
* verse spacing;
* commentary spacing;
* page breaks;
* chapter title pages;
* book title pages;
* ToC spacing;
* footnote behavior;
* glyph handling;
* RTL behavior.

Do not "clean up" magic numbers merely because they are magic numbers.

You may move them into well-named configuration records, which is encouraged.

For example, replacing:

```latex id="7nqdgo"
\vspace{0.73em}
```

with a named configuration value may be excellent.

Changing `0.73em` to `0.75em` because it looks cleaner in the source is not.

---

# Preserve dark-mode semantics

The project has meaningful light/dark behavior.

Preserve it.

In particular, things defined as the default foreground/text color should continue to adapt appropriately between the black-ish light-mode foreground and white-ish dark-mode foreground.

Do not accidentally hard-code black merely because the existing macro resolves to black in the build configuration you happen to test first.

Test both modes if the existing infrastructure supports both.

---

# Preserve source semantics

Source identity is central to the book.

Do not flatten distinctions among authors/sources merely to simplify the implementation.

If `master.tex` distinguishes J, E, P, D, redactors, Aaronid/Mushite categories, special/other material, commentary authors, etc., understand those distinctions and encode them cleanly.

Simplification should make the conceptual model **more explicit**, not less.

---

# Delete historical cruft aggressively, but only after understanding it

Look for:

* duplicate definitions;
* obsolete macros;
* commented-out implementations that no longer matter;
* aliases that are no longer used;
* repeated source-specific implementations differing only by data;
* repeated conditionals;
* duplicated colors/fonts;
* manual lookup tables;
* unnecessary global state;
* TeX constructs simulating records;
* TeX constructs simulating lists;
* TeX constructs simulating dictionaries;
* repeated formatting sequences;
* unnecessary indirection;
* dead compatibility hacks.

Remove or consolidate them where safe.

A major success metric is simply that **there should be much less code**.

But raw line count is secondary to conceptual simplicity.

Do not turn 2,000 readable lines into 500 incomprehensible lines.

---

# Prefer data-driven generic renderers

Whenever several macros differ only by configuration, ask whether they should instead share one implementation.

For example, if:

```latex id="33k7re"
\hJ
\hE
\hP
\hD
```

all fundamentally mean:

> render a Hebrew block using source X's Hebrew profile

then consider one generic implementation plus thin compatibility wrappers.

Likewise for English source blocks, commentary authors, source-key entries, book ToC entries, etc.

The pattern should generally be:

```text id="hnz5f9"
data record
    +
generic renderer
    +
tiny convenient public macro
```

rather than:

```text id="gbmps6"
large bespoke macro
large bespoke macro
large bespoke macro
large bespoke macro
```

---

# Comments and navigation headings should explain architecture, not history

Reorganize section comments/navigation headings so that someone opening `mistress.tex` for the first time can understand it top-to-bottom.

Do not preserve a confusing ordering merely because that is where code happened to accumulate in `master.tex`.

The new file should feel designed.

Use clear major headings corresponding roughly to the hierarchy described above.

---

# Concrete refactoring targets in the existing `master.tex`

The preceding architectural rules are not hypothetical. `master.tex` already contains several places where the underlying conceptual model is clean, but the model has been encoded through layers of TeX macros, generated control-sequence names, duplicated declarations, and conditional plumbing.

When implementing `mistress.tex`, use the following concrete observations about the existing file as refactoring targets.

The purpose is **not** to preserve the internal structure described below. Quite the opposite: preserve its semantics while replacing it with a smaller representation in which:

```text
data is data
logic is logic
rendering is rendering
compatibility macros are thin wrappers
```

Do not move every line to Lua merely because Lua exists. Instead identify the pieces where TeX is presently functioning as a database, object system, enum implementation, or loop language, and replace those with actual data structures.

---

## A. Collapse the configuration bootstrap into one configuration object

At the beginning of `master.tex`, configuration currently starts with values such as:

```latex
\def\ConfigTheme{dark}
\def\ConfigVerseLayout{horizontal}
\def\ConfigOutputMode{mobile}
\def\ConfigCommentary{true}
\def\IndividualBookTitlePageStyle{fancy}
```

and then defines constants such as:

```latex
\def\ConfigTrue{true}
\def\ConfigThemeDark{dark}
\def\ConfigVerseLayoutHorizontal{horizontal}
\def\ConfigVerseLayoutVertical{vertical}
...
```

and then converts those strings into another parallel layer of TeX booleans:

```latex
\newif\ifConfigCommentaryOn
\newif\ifConfigVerseLayoutVertical
\newif\ifConfigOutputModeMobile
\newif\ifIndividualBookTitlePageStyleFancy
```

followed by repeated tests such as:

```latex
\ifx\ConfigOutputMode\ConfigOutputModeMobile
    \ConfigOutputModeMobiletrue
\else
    \ConfigOutputModeMobilefalse
\fi
```

This is precisely the kind of infrastructure that should disappear.

There should be **one source of truth**.

Conceptually:

```lua
book.config = {
    theme = "dark",
    verse_layout = "horizontal",
    output_mode = "mobile",
    commentary = true,
    book_title_page_style = "fancy",
}
```

Then provide extremely small tests from TeX when rendering genuinely depends on those settings.

For example, the public interface could conceptually expose things like:

```latex
\IfBookModeTF{mobile}{...}{...}
\IfThemeTF{dark}{...}{...}
\IfCommentaryTF{...}{...}
```

or use generated/internal conditionals behind the scenes.

What should **not** survive is this chain:

```text
configuration string
    ↓
constant macro
    ↓
\ifx comparison
    ↓
parallel \newif state
    ↓
actual rendering conditional
```

That is four representations of one fact.

Reduce it to one fact and one accessor.

---

## B. Treat output modes as named profiles, not scattered conditionals

`master.tex` contains a substantial distinction between mobile and book rendering:

* different geometry;
* different typography ladders;
* different line-break penalties;
* different hyperlink styling;
* special front-matter vertical centering;
* alphabet-history text sizing;
* possibly further scattered behavior.

Do not implement this as dozens of independent:

```latex
\ifConfigOutputModeMobile
...
\else
...
\fi
```

tests.

Instead define output profiles.

For example:

```lua
book.output_profiles = {
    book = {
        geometry = {
            paperwidth = "7in",
            paperheight = "10in",
            inner = "0.8in",
            outer = "0.45in",
            top = "0.55in",
            bottom = "0.75in",
            twoside = true,
        },

        text_sizes = {
            tiny = {"6pt", "7pt"},
            scriptsize = {"8pt", "9.5pt"},
            ...
        },

        chapter_link = {
            underlined = false,
            color = "TextColor",
        },

        verse_line_break_penalty = -700,
    },

    mobile = {
        geometry = {
            paperwidth = "7in",
            paperheight = "14.68in",
            left = "0.625in",
            right = "0.625in",
            top = "0.55in",
            bottom = "0.75in",
        },

        text_sizes = {
            tiny = {"8pt", "9.33pt"},
            scriptsize = {"10.67pt", "12.67pt"},
            ...
        },

        chapter_link = {
            underlined = true,
            color = "MobileLinkColor",
        },

        verse_line_break_penalty = 3000,
    }
}
```

The exact data representation is flexible.

The important idea is that **mobile mode is a profile**, not a condition independently rediscovered by every subsystem.

Then render/apply the selected profile once where possible.

Some behavior will still require a runtime branch, but it should preferably ask:

```text
what does the active profile specify?
```

rather than:

```text
are we mobile again?
```

This makes adding a third output mode later vastly easier.

