## E. Consolidate Hebrew encoders around a generic mapping engine

The existing `paleotex` Lua section is already one of the healthier parts of `master.tex`, because it uses actual Lua tables for things that are naturally mappings.

Preserve that direction, but simplify it further.

There are currently multiple conversion functions with essentially the same traversal structure:

```lua
hebrew_to_aramaic(...)
hebrew_to_paleo_hebrew_phoenician(...)
hebrew_for_paleo_unicode(...)
hebrew_to_paleo_ascii(...)
hebrew_to_ascii_variant(...)
```

Several of these:

1. iterate through Unicode codepoints;
2. look for a mapping;
3. fall back to punctuation;
4. drop Hebrew marks;
5. preserve unrelated characters.

That traversal should probably exist once.

Conceptually:

```lua
local function map_hebrew(s, mapping, options)
    ...
end
```

Then:

```lua
function paleotex.encode(s, encoder_name)
    local encoder = paleotex.encoders[encoder_name]
    return map_hebrew(s, encoder.map, encoder.options)
end
```

and encoders become declarative records:

```lua
paleotex.encoders = {
    aramaic = {
        map = aramaic_map,
        strip_marks = true,
    },

    phoenician = {
        map = phoenician_map,
        strip_marks = true,
    },

    proto_a = {
        map = ascii_variants.proto_a,
        strip_marks = true,
    },

    raw = {
        strip_marks = false,
        qere_ketiv = true,
    },
}
```

This reduces both Lua code and TeX wrappers.

