## G. Historical Hebrew renderers should be records, not one declaration per Cartesian combination

The file currently has a generic:

```latex
\DeclareHebrewRenderer
```

which is already an abstraction improvement, but it is followed by a very long list:

```latex
\DeclareHebrewRenderer{ProtoAFont}{...}{\EncodeProtoA}
\DeclareHebrewRenderer{ProtoBFont}{...}{\EncodeProtoB}
...
\DeclareHebrewRenderer{PaleoAFont}{...}{\EncodePaleoA}
\DeclareHebrewRenderer{PaleoBFont}{...}{\EncodePaleoB}
...
\DeclareHebrewRenderer{PaleoXFont}{...}{\EncodePaleoX}
```

This should become a renderer registry.

Conceptually:

```lua
book.hebrew_renderers = {
    proto_a = {
        font = "ProtoSinaiticFifteenthCenturyFont",
        encoder = "proto_a",
    },

    proto_b = {
        font = "ProtoSinaiticFifteenthCenturyFont",
        encoder = "proto_b",
    },

    paleo_tel_zayit = {
        font = "PaleoHebrewTelZayitFont",
        encoder = "paleo_ascii",
    },

    ...
}
```

Then there is one generic TeX renderer:

```text
render Hebrew with renderer X and style Y
```

If compatibility macros such as:

```latex
\ProtoAFont
\PaleoAFont
```

are actually used by content files, auto-generate those tiny wrappers from the registry.

The registry should be the source of truth.

