## I. Distinguish inheritance/composition from overrides

Several source styles are composed from traits:

```text
E = Mushite + Priest + E colors/background
P = Aaronid + Priest + P colors/background
DtrA = Mushite-ish + Dtr1 color + Priest + optional redactor background
Ezekiel = Aaronid + Ezekiel color
```

Represent that relationship explicitly rather than flattening everything into TeX macro sequences.

For example:

```lua
book.source_classes = {
    priest = {
        weight = "bold",
    },

    mushite = {
        foreground = "Ecolor",
    },

    aaronid = {
        foreground = "Pcolor",
    },

    poetry = {
        shape = "italic",
    },

    law = {
        english_font = "law",
    },
}
```

Then:

```lua
book.sources.E = {
    traits = {"mushite", "priest"},
    background = "Ebgcolor",
    hebrew_renderer = "paleo_standard",
    hebrew_scale = 1.35,
    english_scale = 0.96,
}
```

The source resolver can merge traits and source-specific overrides.

This is far better than relying on ordering effects such as:

```latex
\MushiteStyle\color{Dtr1color}\PriestStyle
```

where the semantics depend partly on which macro happens to run last.

