## J. Make the author taxonomy real data

The source-key prose already says things like:

```text
Mushites:
    E
    Dtr1
    Dtr2
    Jeremiah

Aaronids:
    P
    R
    Ezekiel
```

This relationship should not exist merely as prose on a front-matter page.

Put it in the source data.

For example:

```lua
book.author_groups = {
    mushite = {
        label = "Mushites",
        members = {"E", "DtrA", "DtrB", "Jeremiah"},
        color = "Ecolor",
    },

    aaronid = {
        label = "Aaronids",
        members = {"P", "R", "Ezekiel"},
        color = "Pcolor",
    },
}
```

Then the source-key page can consume the same taxonomy used by the rendering system.

That gives the project one consistent representation of the idea.

---

## K. Preserve `\eJ`, `\hJ`, etc., but generate them automatically

The existing public source API is good:

```latex
\eJ{...}
\hJ{...}

\eE{...}
\hE{...}

\eP{...}
\hP{...}
```

Keep it.

What should disappear is the machinery required to manually establish `estyleJ`, `hJRenderer`, `hstyleJ`, and then separately call `\DeclareSourceCommands`.

Once the Lua source registry exists, generate compatibility commands automatically for every registered source.

Conceptually:

```text
for each source S:
    define \eS{text} → generic English source renderer(S, text)
    define \hS{text} → generic Hebrew source renderer(S, text)
    define \sS{text} → appropriate source styling
```

Then adding a new source becomes a data operation:

```lua
book.sources.NewGuy = {...}
```

not a five-place TeX declaration followed by adding `NewGuy` to another manual list.

---

## L. Remove the manually synchronized `\DeclareSourceCommandList`

Currently there is a giant list:

```latex
\DeclareSourceCommandList{
    J,E,P,PP,DtrA,DtrB,DtrH,Dtn,Records,Other,X,Aramaic,
    Proto,ProtoA,...,
    Paleo,PaleoA,...,PaleoY
}
```

This is duplicated knowledge.

The project already had to declare those source profiles individually.

The existence of a source profile should itself imply the existence of its public commands.

There should be no second list that must remain synchronized.

This is exactly the kind of accidental bookkeeping Lua can eliminate.

---

## M. Replace `\UsePaleoP` / `\UseSquareP` implementation mutation with source state

The existing P-switching mechanism saves:

```latex
\SquarePhRenderer
\SquarePhStyle
```

and then dynamically mutates:

```latex
\hPRenderer
\hstyleP
```

through `\let`.

Preserve the feature, but represent it as data.

For example:

```lua
book.sources.P.hebrew_variant = "square"
```

with possible renderers:

```lua
book.sources.P.hebrew_variants = {
    square = {...},
    paleo = {...},
}
```

Then:

```latex
\UsePaleoP
```

can simply set:

```lua
book.sources.P.hebrew_variant = "paleo"
```

and:

```latex
\UseSquareP
```

sets it back.

This makes the intent explicit.

---

## N. Collapse color/theme duplication into semantic color tokens

The color section currently first defines a light palette and then, under the dark-theme condition, overwrites many of the same semantic colors:

```latex
Jcolor
Ecolor
Pcolor
Dtr1color
Dtr2color
Jeremiahcolor
Ezekielcolor
...
Jbgcolor
Ebgcolor
Pbgcolor
...
TextColor
RuleColor
...
```

The important insight is that these are **semantic color names**.

Keep those semantic names on the TeX side because `xcolor` needs them, but drive them from theme data.

Conceptually:

```lua
book.themes = {
    light = {
        text = "...",
        rule = "...",
        J = {...},
        E = {...},
        P = {...},
        ...
    },

    dark = {
        text = "...",
        rule = "...",
        J = {...},
        E = {...},
        P = {...},
        ...
    },
}
```

Then one theme application function establishes the semantic `xcolor` aliases once.

The rest of the book should know only:

```text
Jcolor
Jbgcolor
TextColor
RuleColor
```

not which concrete palette is active.

That is already approximately the intended semantic organization of the current code; formalize it.

