## P. Make commentary speakers another profile table

The commentary system currently has:

```latex
\DeclareSpeakerShortcut{A}{\cK}
\DeclareSpeakerShortcut{B}{\cR}
\DeclareSpeakerShortcut{C}{\cB}
\DeclareSpeakerShortcut{J}{\eJ}
\DeclareSpeakerShortcut{E}{\eE}
...
```

followed by another generated layer for:

```text
aAl / aAc / aAr
aBl / aBc / aBr
...
```

This can become a simple speaker registry:

```lua
book.commentators = {
    A = { renderer = "default" },
    B = { renderer = "red" },
    C = { renderer = "blue" },

    J = { source = "J" },
    E = { source = "E" },
    P = { source = "P", english_renderer = "aPEnglish" },
    ...
}
```

Preserve the public commands:

```latex
\aA{}
\aB{}
\aC{}
```

and the source commentary shortcuts where they are used.

But generate them systematically.

Also inspect whether the optional alignment parameter in:

```latex
\as{style}[l|c|r]{text}
```

is genuinely used in `\as` itself. In the current implementation the argument appears to be accepted but not actually consumed by `\as`; alignment is instead implemented in the generated triplet macros.

If that observation survives full usage analysis, simplify the API internally rather than preserving meaningless plumbing.

---

## Q. Commentary A/B/C should use semantic profiles, especially A

Do not encode:

```text
A = black
B = red
C = blue
```

because A is not actually black.

A means:

```text
default foreground text color
```

which is black-ish in light mode and white-ish in dark mode.

So represent:

```lua
A = { color = "TextColor" }
B = { color = "Redcolor" }
C = { color = "Bluecolor" }
```

This preserves dark-mode semantics naturally.

---

## R. The chapter-link / book-ToC system is currently a TeX database; turn it into actual book metadata

The existing book navigation system stores information through dynamically constructed control sequences:

```latex
\DeclareChapterLinkRange{Genesis}{50}
\DeclareChapterLinkRange{Exodus}{40}
...
```

then separate structures for:

```latex
\DeclareBookChapterLinkList
\DeclareBookChapterSummaryLinkList
\DeclareBookExtraLinkList
```

and tests for their existence using:

```latex
\@ifundefined{...}
```

This entire family should be replaced by a book registry.

For example:

```lua
book.books = {
    Genesis = {
        title = "Genesis",
        chapters = 50,
        directory = "01-genesis",
        extras = {
            {kind="book", target="Genesis Apocrypha", label="Genesis Apocrypha"},
        },
    },

    Exodus = {
        chapters = 40,
        directory = "02-exodus",
    },

    Samuel = {
        parts = {
            {name="I Samuel", chapters=31, prefix="1-"},
            {name="II Samuel", chapters=24, prefix="2-"},
        },
    },

    Kings = {
        parts = {
            ...
        },
    },
}
```

Again, adapt this to the actual repository structure rather than copying it blindly.

The key fact is that:

```text
Genesis has 50 chapters
```

should be a field in Genesis's record.

It should not exist as a macro named:

```text
ChapterLinkRange@Genesis
```

---

## S. Chapter summaries are pure content data and should look like pure content data

The enormous block beginning with declarations like:

```latex
\DeclareBookChapterSummaryLinkList{Genesis}{
    \ChapterSummaryLink{Genesis}{1}{Genesis}{Creation}
    \ChapterSummaryLink{Genesis}{2}{Genesis}{Adam and Eve}
    ...
}
```

contains valuable content but almost no program logic.

Represent it accordingly.

Prefer something visually compact like:

```lua
book.chapter_summaries.Genesis = {
    "Creation",
    "Adam and Eve",
    "Snake; exile",
    "Cain and Abel",
    ...
}
```

when chapter numbers are simply the array indices.

If some chapters need richer TeX content such as the specially constructed:

```latex
\IsaacBoundBurnedSummary
```

support an escape mechanism.

For example:

```lua
book.chapter_summaries.Genesis = {
    ...
    [22] = {tex = "\\IsaacBoundBurnedSummary"},
}
```

or keep exceptional summary content as named TeX snippets while the ordinary 99% remain plain data.

Do **not** force every simple summary through a four-argument macro merely because one unusual summary needs TeX.

---

## T. Eliminate repeated book names from chapter-summary declarations

The current Genesis declaration contains repeated information of this general shape:

```latex
\ChapterSummaryLink{Genesis}{1}{Genesis}{Creation}
\ChapterSummaryLink{Genesis}{2}{Genesis}{Adam and Eve}
\ChapterSummaryLink{Genesis}{3}{Genesis}{Snake; exile}
```

The enclosing structure already says Genesis.

The third argument also says Genesis.

The chapter sequence itself supplies the chapter number.

So most rows conceptually contain only one new fact:

```text
the summary
```

Normalize accordingly.

A data representation should avoid repeating facts implied by containment.

---

## U. Derive chapter counts from summary data where safe

For books with complete summary lists, there may be no need to separately state:

```text
Genesis has 50 chapters
```

and then separately store 50 summaries.

If the summary table is authoritative, `#summaries` can provide the chapter count.

However, do this only if it remains semantically correct for books with incomplete content, composite books, apocrypha, or other special structures.

Avoid replacing obvious duplication with a brittle hidden assumption.

---

## V. The main corpus assembly can be generated from book metadata

The bottom of `master.tex` contains hundreds of lines such as:

```latex
\Book{Genesis}
\include{01-genesis/01}
\include{01-genesis/02}
...
\include{01-genesis/50}
\include{01-genesis/apocrypha}

\Book{Exodus}
\include{02-exodus/01}
...
```

This is a perfect example of data that should drive generation.

The assembly metadata can specify:

```lua
{
    title = "Genesis",
    path = "01-genesis",
    chapters = 50,
    after = {"apocrypha"},
}
```

and Lua can emit the equivalent includes.

Composite books can specify parts:

```lua
{
    title = "Samuel",
    path = "08-samuel",
    parts = {
        {title="I Samuel",  prefix="1-", chapters=31},
        {title="II Samuel", prefix="2-", chapters=24},
    },
}
```

Then the final book assembly may become conceptually as small as:

```latex
\RenderBiblicalCorpus
```

or perhaps, if keeping the publication sequence explicitly visible is preferred:

```latex
\RenderBook{Genesis}
\RenderBook{Exodus}
\RenderBook{Leviticus}
...
```

I prefer the latter if it makes the canonical order clearer to a human reader.

The point is to eliminate the hundreds of predictable `\include` lines, not necessarily to hide the order of the books.

---

## W. Preserve exceptional assembly declaratively

Do not make the book registry incapable of expressing:

* Genesis apocrypha;
* Samuel as I/II Samuel;
* Kings as I/II Kings;
* the `Dudetheyreontome` grouping;
* `On the Lamb`;
* Ezra;
* Nehemiah;
* Esther;
* other oddities.

Exceptional structure is not cruft merely because it is exceptional.

Instead, make the data model capable of describing it cleanly.

The result should say:

```text
this book has parts
```

rather than requiring a bespoke sequence of `\Book`, `\BookPart`, and manually prefixed include paths.

---

## X. Simplify the alphabet-history implementation without touching its tuned visual values

The alphabet-history pages currently have two different kinds of data mixed together.

There is reusable rendering logic:

```latex
\FitAlphabetHistorySample
\AlphabetHistoryASCII
\AlphabetHistoryRightToLeft
```

and then there is page data such as:

```latex
font = ProtoSinaiticFifteenthCenturyFont
scale = 1.43
sample = \protoalower
direction = ASCII/reversed
```

repeated line by line.

Separate those.

Conceptually:

```lua
book.alphabet_history.pages = {
    {
        {
            renderer = "egypt",
            sample = "...",
        },
        {
            font = "ProtoSinaiticBestFont",
            scale = 1.81,
            sample = "hebrew_alphabet",
            mode = "ascii_reversed",
        },
        {
            font = "ProtoSinaiticFifteenthCenturyFont",
            scale = 1.43,
            sample = "proto_a_lower",
            mode = "ascii_reversed",
        },
        ...
    },

    {
        ...
    },
}
```

Then use one generic row renderer.

**Preserve every scale, spacing adjustment, manual sample string, negative kern, punctuation hack, and page division unless testing proves it can be replaced exactly.**

The alphabet-history section is a place where the data representation should be simplified but the visual tuning should remain sacred.

---

## Y. Do not incorrectly normalize the alphabet-history sample strings

Strings such as:

```latex
\protoalower
\protoaupper
\protobupper
...
```

contain explicit TeX spacing and kern adjustments:

```latex
\!
\;
\,
```

These appear to be calibration data, not merely alphabet strings.

Treat them as such.

They can live in Lua as TeX-bearing strings if that is robust, or remain named TeX constants stored in a small data registry.

Do not derive a theoretically "clean" alphabet sequence and thereby destroy the manually tuned glyph spacing.

This is an excellent example of the distinction between:

```text
ugly implementation
```

and:

```text
ugly-looking but intentional data
```

Delete the former.

Preserve the latter.

---

## Z. Consolidate title-page geometry and page-shell behavior

The publication title page, contents page, and book cover page repeat a common pattern:

```latex
\clearpage
\newgeometry{...}
\pagestyle{empty}
...
\newpage
\restoregeometry
\pagestyle{fancy}
```

Create a reusable page-shell abstraction.

Conceptually:

```latex
\SpecialPage[
    geometry=title,
    pagestyle=empty,
    restorepagestyle=fancy
]{...}
```

or an internal environment.

Then define:

```latex
\RenderPublicationTitlePage
\RenderContentsPage
\RenderBookTitlePage
```

in terms of it.

Keep the carefully chosen dimensions and vertical positions as data/configuration.

Do not repeat the mechanical geometry lifecycle.

---

## AA. Separate publication title-page content from its layout

The current `\wetitlepage` hard-codes both layout and actual content:

```text
We
The Nameless
```

That should be split.

For example:

```lua
book.binding = {
    title = "We",
    subtitle = "The Nameless",
}
```

and:

```latex
\RenderPublicationTitlePage
```

uses those values.

This directly satisfies the larger infrastructure/content separation requested for `mistress.tex`.

---

## AB. Treat source-key page prose as content attached to source/group records where practical

The source-key page currently manually lays out material such as:

```text
J — Green — prose about J
Mushites — Yellow — prose about Mushites
Aaronids — Blue — prose about Aaronids
Deuteronomists — Orange — prose about Deuteronomists
Editors — Highlights — prose about redactors
Records — Grey — prose about records
```

Not all prose needs to be moved into Lua.

Long TeX-rich paragraphs may remain more comfortably in TeX.

But the **identity and rendering metadata** of the rows should not be restated independently from the source registry.

For instance, the source-key page should be able to ask the model:

```text
What color represents J?
Which sources are Mushites?
Which sources are Aaronids?
What renderer shows a sample of P?
```

rather than manually duplicating those relationships.

Let prose be prose.

Do not let prose become a second database.

---

## AC. Consolidate source-font legend generation

The source-font pages contain repeated rows of the conceptual form:

```text
[source alphabet sample] → [description]
```

The sample side is often derivable directly from a source profile.

For example:

```latex
\LegendFontAlphabet{\hJ}
\LegendFontAlphabet{\hE}
```

This is an excellent place for generic functions such as:

```latex
\SourceAlphabetSample{J}
\SourceAlphabetSample{E}
\SourceAlphabetSample{P}
```

which consult the source registry.

The explanatory paragraphs can remain content.

---

## AD. `\Book`, `\Chapter`, and `\Verse` should become the clean structural spine of the TeX layer

These are good conceptual APIs.

Preserve and simplify them.

At the structural level, a reader should be able to understand the book through commands such as:

```latex
\Book{Genesis}
\Chapter{14}
\Verse{13}{...}{...}{...}
```

even if their internals are radically rewritten.

The implementation of `\Book` should not need to perform database lookups through `\csname` naming conventions if Lua already knows what Genesis is.

The implementation of `\Chapter` should primarily establish chapter-level rendering/navigation state.

The implementation of `\Verse` should primarily orchestrate verse rendering.

These macros should become **orchestrators**, not databases.

---

## AE. Refactor `\Verse` into explicit small rendering stages

The current `\Verse` macro performs:

1. paragraph termination;
2. vertical spacing;
3. `Needspace`;
4. first rule;
5. verse heading;
6. second rule;
7. English/Hebrew body;
8. warning suppression;
9. commentary gating;
10. commentary rendering;
11. more spacing.

That is not terrible, but some of this already duplicates `\VerseHeader`.

Make the conceptual pieces explicit:

```text
Verse
    ├── header
    ├── bilingual body
    │     ├── English block
    │     └── Hebrew block
    └── commentary block
```

The final `\Verse` implementation should look approximately like an orchestration of those pieces.

For example, conceptually:

```latex
\NewDocumentCommand{\Verse}{m +m +m +m}{%
    \VerseBegin{#1}%
    \VerseBlocks{#3}{#2}%
    \VerseCommentary{#4}%
    \VerseEnd
}
```

Do not copy those exact names if better ones emerge.

The goal is that `\Verse` itself communicates the physical structure of a verse.

---

## AF. Make verse layout a renderer selected by configuration, not a nested conditional concern

There are currently:

```latex
\VerseColumns
\VerseVertical
\VerseBody
```

with `\VerseBody` checking the selected layout.

That basic architecture is reasonable.

Take it one step further by treating the layout implementation as a selected renderer:

```text
horizontal → VerseColumns
vertical   → VerseVertical
```

Then selecting `verse_layout` determines one rendering function/macro.

Avoid repeatedly asking about the layout elsewhere.

---

## AG. Consolidate line-break policy into layout/output profiles

The `\nl` macro currently examines both:

```text
verse layout
output mode
```

to choose among:

```text
horizontal desktop penalty
horizontal mobile penalty
vertical penalty
```

This is configuration data.

Resolve the active penalty from the current layout/output profile rather than embedding the decision tree in `\nl`.

Conceptually:

```lua
book.layout.active_line_break_penalty()
```

or calculate/set a TeX count whenever mode/layout changes.

Then `\nl` can become nearly:

```latex
\unskip
\penalty\ActiveVerseLineBreakPenalty
\hskip ...
```

This is exactly the kind of tiny simplification that compounds across a large architecture.

---

## AH. Preserve special page-breaking tools, but keep them independent of unrelated "binding" content

The `together` environment is reusable pagination infrastructure.

It does not conceptually belong to content or book binding merely because it currently appears near that navigation marker.

Move it to a logical place such as:

```text
global pagination utilities
```

or:

```text
page/chunk layout helpers
```

The new navigation should reflect what a mechanism *is*, not where it happened to be added historically.

---

## AI. Replace dynamic-word control-sequence gymnastics with a small lexical registry

The current dynamic-word system implements `\defineword` and `\redefineword` by constructing companion `Init` commands dynamically.

The user-facing idea is useful:

```latex
\God
```

can be globally or locally redefined, while sentence-initial capitalization still works.

Preserve that feature but simplify its representation.

A lexical registry can conceptually store:

```lua
book.words = {
    God = {
        normal = "God",
        initial = "God",
    },
}
```

and setters can modify:

```lua
book.words.God.normal
book.words.God.initial
```

Alternatively, if runtime expansion and grouping semantics make TeX storage much simpler here, use `expl3` property maps rather than forcing Lua.

This is one of the places where **expl3 may actually be preferable** because local TeX grouping of temporary word substitutions can be valuable.

The important requirement is to remove the fragile dynamic companion-control-sequence construction if it buys no essential behavior.

---

## AJ. Redaction jokes are lexical data, not infrastructure

Macros such as:

```latex
\cum
\pussya
\pussyb
\pussy
\dick
\penisa
\penisb
\penisc
\penisd
\penis
\foreskin
```

are content/configuration at the **word level**.

Keep the generic `\redacted` renderer in infrastructure.

Then keep the particular joke expansions together as lexical definitions.

If desired, represent variants as:

```lua
book.words.pussy = {
    active = "a",
    variants = {
        a = [[p\redacted{ussy}\eR{aternit}y]],
        b = [[p\redacted{ussy}\eR{atriarch}y]],
    }
}
```

or retain them as short TeX macros if storing TeX-bearing fragments in Lua is more awkward.

The key separation is:

```text
\redacted = rendering infrastructure
\pussy = content/data
```

---

## AK. The defective/matres dictionaries are already data: absorb them into the main linguistic Lua module

The existing:

```lua
h_defective = {...}
e_defective = {...}
```

tables are exactly the sort of data Lua should hold.

Keep that idea, but avoid scattering several independent `\directlua` blocks through `master.tex`.

Put related linguistic transformations into one module/table namespace:

```lua
book.language.hebrew
book.language.transliteration
book.language.matres
book.language.encoders
```

or similar.

Prefer one coherent Lua subsystem over multiple anonymous global functions such as:

```lua
mark_translit
mark_hebrew
mark_hebrew_defective
mark_translit_defective
strip_hebrew_niqqud
...
```

Avoid polluting Lua's global namespace unnecessarily.

---

## AL. Eliminate compatibility globals in Lua where possible

The existing Lua code sometimes defines namespaced functions and then re-exports them globally:

```lua
function paleotex.strip_hebrew_niqqud(...)
...
end

function strip_hebrew_niqqud(...)
    return paleotex.strip_hebrew_niqqud(...)
end
```

This is unnecessary unless something external relies on the global name.

Prefer:

```lua
book.hebrew.encode(...)
```

with TeX wrappers calling that namespace directly.

If compatibility exports are genuinely needed, keep them intentionally and document why.

---

## AM. Avoid `\csname` as a database mechanism

Several parts of the existing file store records by synthesizing names:

```latex
\csname ChapterLinkRange@#1\endcsname
\csname BookChapterLinkList@#1\endcsname
\csname BookChapterSummaryLinkList@#1\endcsname
\csname estyle#1\endcsname
\csname h#1Renderer\endcsname
\csname hstyle#1\endcsname
```

`\csname` is useful for generated compatibility commands.

It should not be the primary persistent data model.

A useful rule for this refactor is:

> Use dynamic control-sequence names to expose APIs to TeX, not to simulate tables behind those APIs.

The table belongs in Lua or `expl3`.

---

## AN. Avoid parallel registries that must remain synchronized manually

The current file contains several cases where one conceptual object must be mentioned in multiple places:

```text
declare renderer
declare source profile
add source to command-generation list
possibly add source to commentary speaker list
possibly add source to source legend
possibly add source to font legend
```

This is a major source of maintenance complexity.

A good `book.sources` registry should allow as many of these as possible to derive from one record.

For example:

```lua
J = {
    public = true,
    commentary = true,
    show_in_header_legend = true,
    show_in_source_key = true,
    ...
}
```

Do not over-engineer every presentational exception into flags, but eliminate the obvious synchronization burden.

---

## AO. Prefer declarative ordered arrays when order itself is meaningful

Lua tables used as maps do not naturally communicate publication ordering.

For things whose order matters, use arrays:

```lua
book.corpus = {
    "Genesis",
    "Exodus",
    "Leviticus",
    "Numbers",
    ...
}
```

and perhaps separate keyed records:

```lua
book.books.Genesis = {...}
```

Likewise:

```lua
book.source_key_order = {
    "J",
    "mushites",
    "aaronids",
    "deuteronomists",
    "redactors",
    "records",
}
```

This keeps data structured without losing the intentional sequence.

---

## AP. Keep generic rendering macros boring

A central goal of this rewrite is to make the reusable TeX layer almost disappointingly straightforward.

For example, a generic source English renderer should ideally amount to:

```text
look up source profile
select its English font/style
render argument
```

A Hebrew renderer should ideally amount to:

```text
look up source profile
select renderer
encode if necessary
select font/style
render RTL argument
```

A book ToC renderer should ideally amount to:

```text
iterate chapter-summary data
render each row with one layout macro
```

If the generic renderers become hundreds of lines of abstract metaprogramming, the refactor has failed even if the total line count decreases.

---

## AQ. Keep TeX-bearing content out of Lua unless Lua actually benefits from owning it

Do not interpret "data belongs in Lua" dogmatically.

A chapter summary like:

```text
Creation
```

is excellent Lua data.

A numeric scale like:

```text
1.43
```

is excellent Lua data.

A source relationship like:

```text
P belongs to Aaronids
```

is excellent Lua data.

But a large paragraph full of:

```latex
\eP{...}
\textsc{...}
\footnote{...}
```

may be better left as TeX content.

Likewise, finely calibrated alphabet strings containing explicit TeX kern commands may be better represented as named TeX snippets referenced from Lua.

Use the split:

```text
Lua says WHICH thing and WHAT properties it has.
TeX says HOW rich TeX content is typeset.
```

---

## AR. Use one Lua namespace

Do not accumulate globals.

Create one project namespace, for example:

```lua
nameless = {
    config = {},
    themes = {},
    fonts = {},
    encoders = {},
    renderers = {},
    author_groups = {},
    sources = {},
    commentators = {},
    words = {},
    books = {},
    corpus = {},
    frontmatter = {},
}
```

or another concise name appropriate to the project.

Functions should live beneath it:

```lua
nameless.source.get(...)
nameless.source.resolve(...)
nameless.hebrew.encode(...)
nameless.book.get(...)
nameless.config.is(...)
```

Whether all of this remains in `mistress.tex` inside one `luacode*` block or moves to a `mistress.lua`/project Lua module is a secondary decision.

Architecturally, it should behave as one coherent model.

---

## AS. Prefer a separate Lua file if it makes `mistress.tex` read like a book specification

Given the size of the current embedded Lua and the amount of structured metadata that should move into Lua, strongly consider:

```text
mistress.tex
mistress.lua
```

or a similarly named Lua support file.

A good outcome might be:

```text
mistress.lua
    data model
    registries
    lookup functions
    encoding functions
    conditional logic
    metadata

mistress.tex
    package setup
    font declarations
    small Lua bridge
    rendering macros
    content declarations
    publication assembly
```

Do not split files merely for purity.

Do it if it makes `mistress.tex` substantially easier to read.

---

## AT. Use code generation only where it removes boilerplate without hiding behavior

Automatic generation is appropriate for repetitive compatibility APIs:

```text
\eJ
\hJ
\sJ

\eE
\hE
\sE
```

and potentially commentary shortcuts.

It is less appropriate for highly customized page layout where explicit TeX is clearer.

A good rule is:

> Generate names. Do not generate mysteries.

---

## AU. The desired endpoint for the source system

The existing source system currently roughly behaves like:

```text
font declarations
    ↓
encoder declarations
    ↓
renderer declarations
    ↓
trait macros
    ↓
source profile declaration
    ↓
generated estyle/hstyle/renderer macros
    ↓
manual source command list
    ↓
generated eX/hX/sX commands
    ↓
special aliases and overrides
```

The new system should ideally behave more like:

```text
font declarations in TeX

           +

Lua source data:
    source category
    traits
    colors
    background
    English font
    Hebrew renderer
    scales
    optional variants

           ↓

one generic English renderer
one generic Hebrew renderer

           ↓

automatically generated compatibility commands:
    \eJ
    \hJ
    \eE
    \hE
    ...
```

That single refactor could remove a large amount of conceptual duplication.

---

## AV. The desired endpoint for the book/navigation system

The current navigation system roughly behaves like:

```text
declare chapter counts
declare optional custom chapter lists
declare optional book composition
declare extra links
declare hundreds of chapter summaries
store each declaration in generated control-sequence names
query whether those names exist
dispatch among several rendering macros
manually include every chapter file at document bottom
```

The new system should ideally behave like:

```text
book metadata:
    name
    directory
    chapter count / chapter list
    chapter summaries
    parts
    extras

           ↓

generic renderers:
    main ToC
    book title page
    book-specific chapter ToC
    chapter links

           ↓

publication assembly:
    render/include books in declared order
```

Again:

> one model, many renderers.

---

## AW. The desired endpoint for themes/output configuration

The current system roughly behaves like:

```text
string config macros
    ↓
comparison constants
    ↓
derived TeX booleans
    ↓
conditional geometry
    ↓
conditional typography
    ↓
conditional colors
    ↓
conditional links
    ↓
conditional spacing
```

The new system should ideally behave like:

```text
config:
    theme = dark
    output = mobile
    verse_layout = horizontal
    commentary = true

           +

profile tables

           ↓

apply selected theme/output/layout

           ↓

renderers consume semantic settings
```

---

## AX. The desired endpoint for the file as a whole

The current file contains about sixteen thousand lines because content, data, registries, compatibility layers, rendering code, generated-name infrastructure, and publication assembly have accumulated together.

Do not target an arbitrary line count.

But the resulting architecture should make huge stretches of the current file disappear because the same information can be expressed much more directly.

A reader should eventually see something conceptually like:

```text
CONFIG

PACKAGES

FONTS

LUA MODEL / DATA BRIDGE

AUTHOR GROUPS
SOURCE PROFILES

PUBLICATION LAYOUT
BOOK LAYOUT
CHAPTER LAYOUT
PAGE LAYOUT
VERSE LAYOUT
BLOCK LAYOUT
COMMENTARY LAYOUT
WORD-LEVEL LAYOUT

============================
CONTENT
============================

BINDING / TITLE CONTENT

MAIN TOC CONTENT

ALPHABET HISTORY CONTENT

SOURCE KEY CONTENT

BOOK + CHAPTER SUMMARY DATA

CORPUS ASSEMBLY
```

The code in the first half should define **how**.

The second half should state **what**.

